# Can a Planes list be walked with an explicit stop-early, resume-later
# cursor -- the shape a recursive-descent parser needs (consume some
# prefix, return what's left) -- or only with for each's single,
# uninterruptible full pass? No `rest`/`drop`/index exists for lists
# (declined at #11, vindicated for the lexer's fold design). The
# candidate idiom: convert the list to a cons-list of records once
# (`{ head: x, rest: <cons-list-or-nothing> }`), since RECORD field
# access has no such limit -- `.rest` is plain field access, not
# indexing, and lexer.planes's own indent stack already proves this
# shape works for a stack. Does it work for a forward-walking cursor too?

to to-cons of items:
  give reverse-into of items, nothing

# for each only ever walks forward, wrapping the previous state at each
# step, so this builds the cons-list in REVERSE (the last item seen is
# the outermost cell) -- built with for each and plus's already-proven
# shape, not the cursor idiom this file is establishing.
to reverse-into of items, acc:
  result = acc
  for each x in items:
    result = { head: x, rest: result }
  give unreverse of result, nothing

# Un-reverse by walking the reversed cons-list itself via ordinary
# recursion on .rest (not for each -- a cons-list is not a Planes list,
# for each cannot walk it), rebuilding head-first.
to unreverse of node, acc:
  if node is nothing:
    give acc
  else:
    give unreverse of node.rest, { head: node.head, rest: acc }

# The cursor operations a parser needs: look at the current item without
# consuming it, and consume-and-advance -- both ordinary field access,
# no indexing.
to current of cursor:
  give cursor.head

to advance of cursor:
  give cursor.rest

to at-end of cursor:
  give cursor is nothing

# A tiny "parser": consume every item, building a new plain Planes list
# via plus (proving the cons-list interoperates with the list type the
# rest of the language uses) -- while stopping and resuming one item at
# a time, the exact shape parse_expr/parse_statement need.
to drain of cursor, acc:
  if at-end of cursor:
    give acc
  else:
    item = current of cursor
    give drain of (advance of cursor), (acc plus item)

items = [10, 20, 30, 40]
c = to-cons of items
show current of c
c2 = advance of c
show current of c2
show at-end of c2

drained = drain of c, []
show drained
show count of drained
